      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "likeCount": 0,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": "5d263a7dd1eb70412d64edca",
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "5d23eafda89be6415d30391b",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "CORRECTION: Audio Sources on avatars will NOT have a VRC_SpatialAudioSource added if it does not have one. Attempting to do so causes issues. We STRONGLY recommend using a SpatialAudioSource to control your audio properly.\n\nIf an avatar audio source does not have a VRC_SpatialAudioSource paired, the Audio Source will have a few things forced on it. This is documented here: https://docs.vrchat.com/v2019.2.5/docs/vrc_spatialaudiosource#section-avatar-limitations\n\nBut I'll also copy and paste:\nIf a VRC_SpatialAudioSource component is not paired with an Audio Source on an avatar, the Audio Source is heavily penalized.\n\nThe spatialization curve is forced to 0 -> min(40m, maxDistance) with an inverse-square falloff. Gain is forced to zero, and spatialization/spatialBlend is forced to 3D.\n\nIf you wish to utilize the full settings available for avatar audio, you must use a SpatialAudioSource.\n\n-----\n\nFor avatar audio, we always force volumetric radius to zero.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
